---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import LinkCards from "../../components/docs/LinkCards.astro";
import { docHref } from "../../data/docs";
---

<DocsLayout slug="work">
  <p>Each turn's work sits between your message and the agent's reply. While the agent works, you read what it does, a line at a time. When it finishes, the work folds into one row that says what the turn came to, and a card lists the files it changed.</p>

  <section aria-labelledby="while-it-works">
    <h2 id="while-it-works">While the agent works</h2>
    <p>Under your message, the agent's work reads as lines in the conversation. Once the turn has something to report, a row above the work says what it has come to so far. Click the row to fold the work away. What the agent is doing now then stays visible as a single line. At the turn's end, a line says where it stands: <strong>Working</strong> with a clock, such as <strong>Working 12s</strong>, then <strong>Writing</strong> once the reply starts, and <strong>Worked for 41s</strong> when it's done, beside <strong>Copy</strong> and <strong>Fork</strong>. A turn names its agent only when the agent changed from the turn before, and never the model, which the composer shows. To end the turn early, click <strong>Stop</strong> in the composer.</p>
    <p>The work is told in steps. A step starts when the agent says what it's about to do, and those words head the step. With Claude Code, that's the sentence it writes before a batch of tool calls. With Codex, it's usually the title of its reasoning. When the agent says nothing first, the step is named after what it did, such as <strong>Read 3 files, ran 2 commands</strong>.</p>
    <p>Under a step's head, each thing the agent did is one line, in the order it happened: an icon, a verb and what it was done to, and at the line's end how it came out, such as the lines an edit added and removed or what a search found. The line the agent is on shows the moving cube and stays open, and the lines before it fold to a line each. Searches made one after another share a line that opens to each of them, and several edits to one file read as one change.</p>
    <p>Each line starts with a verb for what was done, and opens to the thing itself:</p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Verb</th>
            <th scope="col">What the line opens to</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Read</strong> or <strong>Listed</strong></td>
            <td>The files the agent read or the folders it listed. Past six, the rest wait behind a count. A line for one file is a link to that file, and pointing at it shows the lines the agent read.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Edited</strong> or <strong>Wrote</strong></td>
            <td>The lines the edit took out and put in. The line ends with the count of lines added and removed. <strong>Wrote</strong> marks a new file.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Searched</strong></td>
            <td>What the agent looked for, then the first lines it found. Each line opens its file at that line. A web search lists the pages it found.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Ran</strong></td>
            <td>The last lines the command printed, then the command as the steps it ran and the folder it ran in. The line names the command by the agent's own words for it or by the command itself.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Tested</strong></td>
            <td>A test run as one cell per test, with the count passed and failed, what ran, and how long it took. A failing test is named under it. OpenOrc reads the output of Vitest, Jest, pytest, Go, and Cargo.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Thought</strong></td>
            <td>The agent's reasoning for the step, when it shared it. The line gives the first of it and how long it took.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Committed</strong>, <strong>Pushed</strong>, or <strong>Opened</strong></td>
            <td>Git work as the branch it happened on: the commits as dots on a line from the base, the push to <code>origin</code> filling the line as it sends, and the pull request it opened.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Other lines appear when the agent does other things, such as <strong>Read</strong> for a web page it fetched, or the name of an MCP server whose tools it used. Subagents get a card of their own. See <a href={docHref("messaging") + "#subagents"}>Subagents</a>.</p>
    <p>When the agent keeps a to-do list, the work's head reads <strong>Plan</strong> with the step it's on, such as <strong>Step 2 of 5</strong>, over a progress bar, and the items check off as the agent works through them. Before the first step, the live line reads <strong>Thinking</strong> while the agent reasons.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="open-a-step">
    <h2 id="open-a-step">Open a step</h2>
    <p>Click a line to open what's behind it, and click it again to fold it back to the line:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A file the agent read opens beside the conversation.</li>
      <li>A search lists what it found, and each result opens its file at the line it matched.</li>
      <li>A command shows the last lines it printed. <strong>earlier lines</strong>, with their count, swaps them for the whole call and its output.</li>
      <li>A tool's line opens the call itself.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A step named after what it did, or after the agent's reasoning, folds when you click its name. Folded, it counts what it did, such as <strong>read 3 files, ran 2 commands</strong>. A step headed by the agent's own sentence stays open.</p>
    <p>In an open call, <strong>Raw call</strong> shows the exact input and output. When a tool's output was very large, OpenOrc keeps a short digest of it, so the row can still say what the call came to after the full output is gone from the page.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="when-it-finishes">
    <h2 id="when-it-finishes">When a turn finishes</h2>
    <p>The head says how long the turn took, or <strong>Failed after</strong> or <strong>Stopped after</strong> and the time. Under it, one row says what the turn came to, and the steps fold away. Click the row to open them again.</p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Item</th>
            <th scope="col">What it says</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Thought 8s</strong></td>
            <td>How long the agent spent reasoning, when it shared its reasoning.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>#218 open</strong></td>
            <td>The pull request the turn opened, or how its last commit or push went.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>+21 −8 in 3 files</strong></td>
            <td>The lines added and removed, and how many files changed.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>12 passed</strong></td>
            <td>The last test run, counted when the test runner printed the counts. Another check, such as <strong>typecheck failed</strong>, shows when there was no counted test run or the check failed.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>2 agents</strong> or <strong>3 sources</strong></td>
            <td>For a turn that changed nothing, the subagents it ran and the web pages it read.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>An item with nothing to report is left out. A turn with nothing else to report says how many steps it took. The sidebar gives you the same news without opening the thread. A thread's row leads with a cube of dots that moves while its agent works, turns amber while it waits for your approval or your answer, and shows whole while a finished turn waits for you to read it.</p>
    <h3 id="timeline">The timeline</h3>
    <p>A turn that edited files has <strong>Timeline</strong> under its work. It draws what the turn did along one line, in the order it happened, and each file the turn edited has a lane of its own, named at its start. Point at a mark, or press <kbd>←</kbd> and <kbd>→</kbd>, to read that step's line under the drawing, and click to keep it open. Click a file's name to go to its last step. <strong>Summary</strong> goes back to the lines.</p>
    <h3 id="proof">Proof beside the reply</h3>
    <p>Once a turn is done, a sentence of its reply that says what the turn did ends in a small pill for what backs it: an edited file with its lines added and removed, such as <strong>WorkActs.tsx +6 −1</strong>, or a check's result, such as <strong>1055 passed</strong>. Click the pill to open that work under the sentence. A check that passed before the turn's last edit says <strong>before the last edit</strong>. When the reply says a check passed that the turn didn't run, the pill says so instead, such as <strong>No test run this turn</strong>.</p>
    <p>A commit's id in a reply is a link. It opens the commit's page on GitHub once <code>origin</code> has the commit, and otherwise the commit in the thread's <strong>Commits</strong> tab.</p>
    <p>
      A reply can show images, such as a screenshot the agent took, and they open full size when you click them. It can also hold a visualization: a diagram, chart, or small interactive explanation that the agent draws with OpenOrc's <code>visualize</code> tool. A visualization runs in a sandbox of its own with no network access, and stays in the conversation's history.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="changes">
    <h2 id="changes">Review or undo a turn</h2>
    <p>A turn that changed files ends with a change card. It reads <strong>Changed 3 files</strong>, with the lines added and removed. Click the chevron to list the files.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Click a file, or <strong>Review</strong> for all of them, to read the turn's diff in the <strong>Changes</strong> tab.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Undo</strong>, then <strong>Confirm undo</strong> within four seconds, to put the files back as they were before the turn.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Undo is unavailable while the agent is working, and for older turns that have no starting checkpoint. In a folder that isn't a Git repository, turns get no change card at all.</p>
    <p>When files change between turns, a line between the turns says so. It reads <strong>You changed 2 files</strong> when you made the edits, or <strong>2 files changed between turns</strong> when OpenOrc can't tell who did. It has its own <strong>Undo</strong>, which puts the files back as they were after the last turn.</p>
    <Callout tone="warning" title="Undo rolls back the whole folder">
      Undo returns the thread's folder to how it was before that turn. Every change made since is undone and new files are deleted, including later turns, your own edits, and work by other threads in the same folder. Files Git ignores are left alone. OpenOrc first saves the files as a checkpoint in the <strong>Checkpoints</strong> tab, so you can bring them back with <strong>Restore</strong>.
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="questions-and-approvals">
    <h2 id="questions-and-approvals">Questions and approvals</h2>
    <p>An agent can stop to ask you something. The question docks onto the top of the composer, with its options, or a text field when there are none.</p>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>Click an option. When the agent asked one question with one answer, that sends it. To answer in your own words, type in the <strong>Something else</strong> field and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>.</li>
      <li>When you can pick several options, or the agent asked several questions at once, pick, then click <strong>Next</strong> for the next question and <strong>Answer</strong> to send them all. Each answer you gave stays above as a line you can click to change.</li>
      <li><strong>Decline</strong> closes the question without an answer.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>A question waits for you in every mode, Autonomous included. When it asks for something secret, the field hides what you type, and the conversation shows <strong>Answer hidden</strong> afterwards.</p>
    <p>An approval docks onto the composer the same way. It shows the command or tool the agent wants to run and offers <strong>Allow once</strong>, <strong>Allow for this run</strong> and <strong>Deny</strong>, or only <strong>Allow once</strong> and <strong>Deny</strong> for a request that can't be allowed for longer, such as a password. For a tool, <strong>Show request</strong> shows the whole request. To deny it and tell the agent what to do instead, type in <strong>Say what to do instead</strong> and click <strong>Deny and send</strong>. Which requests ask first depends on the thread's mode. See <a href={docHref("permissions") + "#approvals"}>Modes and permissions</a>.</p>
    <p>The turn waits until you answer. When more than one request waits, the composer shows one and says how many more wait.</p>
    <p>Once answered, a question stays in the conversation as its record: what was asked, the options with yours marked, and a line such as <strong>Sent to Claude</strong> or <strong>Declined</strong>. An approval you allowed shows as its line in the turn's work, and one you denied leaves a <strong>Denied</strong> line.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="waiting">
    <h2 id="waiting">What waits on you</h2>
    <p>What waits on you shows where it lives. A thread waiting for your approval or your answer leads its row in the sidebar with an amber cube, and its project's name counts it, even while the project is folded. Tasks that wait on you, such as one an agent proposed or one ready for review, count on <strong>Tasks</strong> in the sidebar and lead its list.</p>
    <Callout>Desktop notifications cover the same moments when you're looking at something else: a finished turn, a question, an approval, or a failure. Turn them off in <a href={docHref("settings") + "#general"}>Settings</a>.</Callout>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="next-steps">
    <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
    <LinkCards cards={[{ slug: "permissions" }, { slug: "review" }, { slug: "threads" }, { slug: "tasks" }]} />
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
